1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,033
Price made a new high, 2671. Then no buyer showed up there.

2
00:00:11,484 --> 00:00:15,690
Volume less than half of normal. And price back inside.

3
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:22,331
Today: the marks of an unfinished auction, and why the market comes back there.

4
00:00:23,331 --> 00:00:28,085
Hello, this is episode twenty of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

5
00:00:28,534 --> 00:00:33,681
Last time: trapped traders, and how they become fuel for price.

6
00:00:34,131 --> 00:00:42,099
Today: marks of unfinished business inside the profile. This ties the auction from episode two to the profile from episode seven.

7
00:00:43,099 --> 00:00:51,301
Remember, the market is an auction. Price goes up to find sellers, down to find buyers.

8
00:00:51,751 --> 00:00:55,957
An auction is finished when one side has nobody left.

9
00:00:56,407 --> 00:01:00,848
If people are still standing on both sides, the auction is unfinished.

10
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:10,128
Our example, one gold futures session. Value area 2652 to 2664, POC 2658.

11
00:01:10,579 --> 00:01:19,250
The session high is 2668. But at the very top price, 340 sold and 310 bought.

12
00:01:19,701 --> 00:01:27,172
So even at the top, both sides traded heavily. That is a poor high: a flat top, no tail.

13
00:01:28,172 --> 00:01:35,982
The opposite is excess. Look at another day's high: a thin tail from 2672 to 2676.

14
00:01:36,433 --> 00:01:43,747
Only 5 to 20 contracts at each price. Price went there, buyers ran out, and it snapped back.

15
00:01:44,196 --> 00:01:50,231
That is a finished auction. A poor high is unfinished, excess is finished.

16
00:01:51,231 --> 00:01:58,154
Now single prints. Price ran from 2645 to 2655 in a single half hour.

17
00:01:58,604 --> 00:02:05,683
In the TPO profile, 2646 to 2654 has only one letter. No pause in between.

18
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:13,055
It is a thin zone. If price comes back, it either moves through it fast or finds support there.

19
00:02:14,055 --> 00:02:19,463
Why does the market come back to unfinished business? Because orders are still left there.

20
00:02:19,912 --> 00:02:26,103
Above a poor high sit stops and breakout traders. The liquidity pool from episode seventeen.

21
00:02:26,554 --> 00:02:31,230
But remember: it often comes back, not always.

22
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:38,656
Next day, price went above 2668, up to 2671. The poor high got repaired.

23
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:45,454
But volume in that half hour was only 600 contracts. Normally around 2,000.

24
00:02:45,904 --> 00:02:52,173
No new buyers came. The close was back at 2666. That is a failed auction.

25
00:02:53,173 --> 00:02:59,129
The plan idea: entry at 2666 after a close back below 2667.

26
00:02:59,579 --> 00:03:05,613
Stop above the failure high, at 2672.5. Risk 6.5 points.

27
00:03:06,063 --> 00:03:12,594
Target the bottom of the value area, 2652. 14 points, about 2R.

28
00:03:13,594 --> 00:03:22,109
When is the idea wrong? If price gives two half-hour closes above 2668, that is acceptance above. Get out.

29
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:28,672
Not every poor high becomes a failed auction. Often the market goes up and stays there.

30
00:03:29,122 --> 00:03:34,843
And in forex, this profile is built from tick volume. The real picture is on futures.

31
00:03:35,843 --> 00:03:44,046
So, today in short. A poor high or low: heavy two-sided trade at the top, no tail. Unfinished business.

32
00:03:44,495 --> 00:03:51,497
Excess: a thin tail, a sharp rejection. Single prints: the thin zone of a fast move.

33
00:03:51,947 --> 00:03:57,197
Failed auction: a new high, but low volume and back inside.

34
